## What happened

**macOS's native crypto framework (Security.framework) has never supported DSA.** In .NET 10 and earlier, the runtime included an OpenSSL-based fallback on macOS to fill this gap. In .NET 11, that fallback was removed — `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS.

Windows (CNG) and Linux (OpenSSL) still work, but DSA APIs are marked obsolete (`SYSLIB0030`) across all platforms and are on the path to full removal.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Migrate away from DSA (preferred)**
If you control both signing and verification, switch to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**. DSA is a legacy algorithm with known weaknesses at small key sizes.

**Option B — Use BouncyCastle for cross-platform DSA verification (if you must verify existing signatures)**

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Signers;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var publicKey = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var signer = new DsaSigner();
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);

    // Hash the message first (DSA signs a hash, typically SHA-1/SHA-256)
    var digest = DigestUtilities.GetDigest("SHA-256");
    digest.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    var hash = new byte[digest.GetDigestSize()];
    digest.DoFinal(hash, 0);

    // Decode the DER-encoded signature into (r, s)
    var derSeq = (Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.Asn1Sequence)
        Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.Asn1Object.FromByteArray(signature);
    var r = ((Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.DerInteger)derSeq[0]).Value;
    var s = ((Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.DerInteger)derSeq[1]).Value;

    return signer.VerifySignature(hash, r, s);
}
```

BouncyCastle is a pure managed implementation — it works identically on Linux, Windows, and macOS with no native crypto dependency.

**Option C — Suppress the platform gap with OpenSSL on macOS**
Install OpenSSL via Homebrew (`brew install openssl`) and set `DOTNET_SYSTEM_SECURITY_CRYPTOGRAPHY_USEOPENSSL=1` — but this is fragile, not officially supported long-term, and ties your deployment to Homebrew.

---

**Recommendation:** Go with **Option B** if you need to keep verifying legacy DSA signatures, and plan a migration to ECDSA/Ed25519 (**Option A**) for any new signing.
